{
 "app_version": "7.5.20",
 "doc": "Govee's public product catalogue, cut to each product's sku, goodsType and ic (no names, images or URLs). Source: GET https://app2.govee.com/bi/rest/devices/v3/skus, the app's ICategoriesApi, which answers with only an AppVersion header and no account; fetched on the date in `fetched`, with the AppVersion header in `app_version`, by tools/fetch_catalog.py, which rewrites only `app_version`, `fetched` and `skus` (the answer depends on it: on 2026-10-19 the endpoint listed 632 SKUs at 7.7.02 and 617 at 7.5.20). goodsType: the app puts every catalogue product's goodsType, 0 included, into its SKU -> goodsType map (CategoriesDataManager.n -> Pact.j), and a scan takes the SKU's goodsType from that map, falling back to the built-in table only for a SKU the map lacks (Pact.f -> Constant4L5.hasKnowSupportGoodsType, whose default is 0). goodsType 0 is the legacy code, not a route: a scan at goodsType 0 parses no advertised pact and re-derives the device by its SKU (BaseBleProcessor.c: the SKU-keyed OldDreamColorUtil / OldBulbUtil parsers; BaseBleProcessor.g: no protocol), and the device list rewrites 0 by SKU and pact or version (devices.yaml assigned_goods_type, from_goods_type [0]). So a catalogue goodsType 0 never routes, and is never devices.yaml's goodsType-0 row (H6188's, which its SKU routes). Precedence: devices.yaml wins where a client_profile row lists the SKU. A non-zero catalogue goodsType that none of the SKU's rows has is a conflict, settled only where an assigned_goods_type item cites the app's rewrite of it to the row's goodsType (an item whose from_goods_type names the catalogue goodsType, or, with no from_goods_type, one that the pact conditions; an unconditioned pairing write cites none); an unsettled one leaves the SKU unrouted, for review. A SKU no row lists routes to the BLE row of its catalogue goodsType. ic: the product's default IC count (Product.ic), which the app reads by SKU in CategoriesConfig.getDefIcNum (0 for a SKU it lacks). Nothing reads it unless a row's app trace cites that read, and no segment count or ic_pieces is derived from it.",
